I built a public-data atlas to ask: where in Nigeria is specialist care missing, what exactly is missing, who can pay, and what would one centre deliver?

What I found:
• Hospitals are spread very unevenly: {fac_high_state} has about {fac_ratio}x as many per person as {fac_low_state}.
• Published counts don't agree. National dialysis centre counts run from {k_centres_min} to {k_centres_max}+. Working radiotherapy machines: {c_work_min} to {c_work_max}, against {c_need} needed.
• When sources say why a unit fails, it's most often {k_top_gap}: power, water, consumables, maintenance. Not just missing machines.
• The states with the fewest hospitals are also the least able to pay (rank correlation {r_gap_pay}).
• One illustrative dialysis centre would treat {k_patients} people, but at public prices it receives {k_price} a session and needs {k_breakeven} to break even.

The biggest lesson: public data tells you where to look first. It can't tell you what works on the ground. That check has to happen in person.

